<DocsLayout
  editPath="src/pages/docs/reference/init.astro"
  title="init"
  description="Prepare the Strata home directory and print the first commands worth running. Optional: every other command works without it."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>init</h1>
  <p>
    <code>strata init</code> creates the Strata home directory and prints the commands worth running first.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata init</CliBlock>
  <CliBlock variant="output"
    >initialized /home/you/.strata next steps strata ./my-db kv put greeting hello strata ./my-db kv
    get greeting strata --cache strata agents guide</CliBlock
  >
  <p>
    Run it again and it says <code>already initialized</code>, followed by the same next steps. It
    does not fail, and it does not overwrite anything.
  </p>

  <h2>You do not have to run it</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part worth knowing. <code>init</code> is a convenience, not a prerequisite: creating a
    database with <code>strata ./my-db kv put greeting hello</code> works on a machine where <code
      >init</code
    > has never been run. Nothing gates on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What it gives you is a home directory in place before anything needs it, and a printed starting
    point.
  </p>

  <h2>What it actually creates</h2>
  <p>
    The home directory, and nothing inside it. There is no configuration file, no database and no
    state: a freshly initialized home is an empty directory. What you find in a home that has been
    used is put there by something else - the installer writes <code>bin/</code>, and <a
      href="/docs/reference/doctor">doctor</a
    > reports a models path that only exists once a local model has been pulled.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>STRATA_HOME</code> moves it. That is how you keep a throwaway home for a test without touching
    the real one.
  </p>
  <CliBlock>STRATA_HOME=/tmp/throwaway strata init</CliBlock>

  <h2>Where the home sits, and what else knows about it</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/reference/doctor">doctor</a> prints the resolved home, which is the quickest way to
    find out which one a shell is using:
  </p>
  <CliBlock>strata doctor</CliBlock>
  <p>
    The user configuration file is separate and is not created by <code>init</code>. <code
      >doctor</code
    > reports its path and whether it is readable.
  </p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>
    <code>init</code> takes no options of its own beyond the <a
      href="/docs/reference/global-options">global ones</a
    >. <code>--json</code> returns the resolved home, whether this call created it, and the next steps
    as an array, which is the form to use if you are scripting a first-run check.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
